  <reporting-org ref="GB-CHC-202918" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam GB</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consolidating Gains for Improved Mining and Petroleum Governance in Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">By 2026 this project will contribute to promote accountability through citizen activism. More specifically, there would be several major activities. Firstly, there would be a conduct of a training for local level legislators (sub -committee members) on petroleum governance looking at various laws; community rights and engagement; environment; local content and gender issues. Others would focus on women empowerment and gender trainings targeting only women and would centre on equipping them with knowledge and experiences of industry players. Learning and exchange sessions would be held for Community Environmental Monitoring and Advocacy Groups (CEMAGs), partners and academia for networking and advocacy purposes. To consolidate Oxfam&apos;s efforts around value -for -money, efforts would also be seen around open contracting, advocacy around extractive resource revenue management particularly the mineral revenue management bill. To connect local -to -national advocacy to regional work, the project would facilitate the conduct of inter - Civil Society Organisation (CSO) and private -sector exchanges, sharing and research work. The overall aim is to promote dialogue; ensure government answers to citizen&apos;s needs and rights to resource revenues as well as to national commitments to regional and international commitments. To follow up on election campaign promises by government in 2016, the Extractives Industries (EI) programme seeks to initiate interventions that would engage government through the youth to ask the right questions about the status of implementation of public commitments including the passage of laws on anti -corruption and mineral revenue management. Central to this project would be to further the agenda on livelihoods through the &#x2018;year of agric&apos; initiative that seek to highlight and project women and youth livelihoods particularly in host mining communities. The ultimate aim is to set an agenda of citizen activism and involvement of women in shaping extractive sector</narrative>
  </description>
